course: dictionary
lesson: 10337
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning. The sentence around it tells you which meaning the writer means. «It is mid-December, nearly dark, and Rat and Mole are tramping home across frosty fields after a long day out with Otter.» Rat and Mole are walking a long way home over fields, so tramping means walking.
explain: Ask yourself what would fit. They are walking home after a long day out, so tramping means walking with steady, heavy steps over a long way. Now swap your meaning into the sentence. «Rat and Mole are walking home across frosty fields» still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
explain: Be careful not to jump to the wrong meaning. «Tramping home» is about walking, not about having no home. Older stories sometimes use tramp for a poor wanderer, but today people say «homeless person». The words around the word show you which meaning is in use.
explain: Some words change meaning across countries. In New Zealand, «tramping» is the normal word for hiking in the bush or mountains. So if a New Zealander says they are going tramping, they mean a walk on a track, not a walk to the shops.
fact: Tramp can be a noun (a tramp, tramps) or a verb (tramp, tramped, tramping).
fact: As a verb, «tramp» means to walk with heavy, loud steps, as in «tramping through mud».
fact: As a noun, «a tramp» can mean a long walk, especially in the countryside.
fact: In New Zealand, «tramping» means hiking in the bush or mountains.
fact: «A tramp» can also mean a person with no home or job who wanders from place to place. Today people say «homeless person» instead.
exercise: «It is mid-December, nearly dark, and Rat and Mole are tramping home across frosty fields.» What does tramping mean here? | Sleeping in the field | *Walking steadily, a long way home | Dancing with joy
exercise: «They tramped through the mud until their boots were brown.» What does tramped mean in this sentence? | Flew high over the mud | *Walked with heavy, loud steps | Sang softly to themselves | Sat down to rest
exercise: In New Zealand, Aroha says, «We are going tramping in the bush this weekend.» What does tramping mean? | Riding a bike around town | Swimming in a lake | *Hiking in the bush or mountains | Going to the shops for food
test: «The old story tells of a tramp who wandered from town to town with no home.» What does tramp mean in this story? | A bright, shining light | A ship that carries goods | *A person with no home or job who wanders | A loud ride on a horse
test: Which word means a long walk in the countryside, like a hike? | Curt | *Tramp | Drooped | Famished
exam: «Rat and Mole tramped home across frosty fields.» In this sentence, tramped means what? | *Walked a long way with steady, heavy steps | Ran in circles to keep warm | Slept outside under the stars | Lived in a home without a job
